1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,178
The question was simple: does this need a synthetic-media disclosure, a caption

2
00:00:05,178 --> 00:00:10,007
telling the viewer plainly that the person on screen isn't real, before it goes

3
00:00:10,007 --> 00:00:11,596
anywhere near an audience?

4
00:00:11,596 --> 00:00:12,024
It did.

5
00:00:12,024 --> 00:00:15,263
Adding it took less time than reading this paragraph.

6
00:00:15,263 --> 00:00:20,214
Back in Section 4.1, that flag was a line item, one more thing to check before an

7
00:00:20,214 --> 00:00:21,436
avatar clip shipped.

8
00:00:21,436 --> 00:00:25,776
It deserves its own lecture now, because the ground underneath it moved

9
00:00:25,776 --> 00:00:27,243
substantially this year.

10
00:00:27,243 --> 00:00:32,132
As of this recording, Meta requires disclosure on AI-generated or AI-modified ad

11
00:00:32,132 --> 00:00:35,494
content as a matter of enforced policy, not a courtesy.

12
00:00:35,494 --> 00:00:39,283
TikTok requires the same for anything significantly AI-edited.

13
00:00:39,283 --> 00:00:42,278
And this is no longer only a platform preference.

14
00:00:42,278 --> 00:00:47,168
Regulators have started writing some version of the same requirement into actual

15
00:00:47,168 --> 00:00:47,412
law.

16
00:00:47,412 --> 00:00:52,240
What does that mean for you, given everything you've built since Section three?

17
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:57,252
Almost every asset in your library qualifies: a generated static, an AI avatar, an

18
00:00:57,252 --> 00:00:58,902
AI-touched-up product shot.

19
00:00:58,902 --> 00:01:00,430
This isn't a corner case.

20
00:01:00,430 --> 00:01:05,198
It's the default condition of the creative this course has taught you to make.

21
00:01:05,198 --> 00:01:08,682
That's not a reason for alarm, it's a reason for a habit.

22
00:01:08,682 --> 00:01:13,693
Treated as the default assumption, the way this lecture shows you, disclosure is a

23
00:01:13,693 --> 00:01:18,461
five-second addition to a workflow you've already built, not a redesign of it.

24
00:01:19,011 --> 00:01:22,454
Notice the direction this table moves in, left to right.

25
00:01:22,454 --> 00:01:26,696
The platform rows are policy, enforced by rejection and account risk.

26
00:01:26,696 --> 00:01:30,324
The regulation rows are law, enforced by an actual penalty.

27
00:01:30,324 --> 00:01:35,427
That's the genuinely new development this year: the same underlying idea, don't let

28
00:01:35,427 --> 00:01:40,346
a viewer mistake synthetic content for something real, has started showing up in

29
00:01:40,346 --> 00:01:43,297
statute, not just a platform's terms of service.

30
00:01:43,297 --> 00:01:46,187
Ask yourself which row actually applies to you.

31
00:01:46,187 --> 00:01:51,291
If your audience is anywhere near New York, or if you're running ads that reach the

32
00:01:51,291 --> 00:01:56,455
EU, the right-hand rows aren't hypothetical, they're your actual exposure, on top of

33
00:01:56,455 --> 00:01:59,222
whatever platform policy already asks of you.

34
00:01:59,222 --> 00:02:04,018
Check where your audience actually is before you assume only the platform rows

35
00:02:04,018 --> 00:02:05,432
matter to your account.

36
00:02:05,432 --> 00:02:10,044
Notice, too, that these rows don't require you to run separate creative per

37
00:02:10,044 --> 00:02:10,843
jurisdiction.

38
00:02:10,843 --> 00:02:15,885
A disclosure caption honest enough to satisfy New York's law is also honest enough

39
00:02:15,885 --> 00:02:20,988
to satisfy Meta's policy and the EU's transparency rules, because all four rows are

40
00:02:20,988 --> 00:02:25,476
converging on roughly the same underlying ask: tell a viewer plainly when

41
00:02:25,476 --> 00:02:26,829
something's synthetic.

42
00:02:26,829 --> 00:02:31,994
Build to the strictest row you're actually exposed to, and the looser ones take care

43
00:02:31,994 --> 00:02:32,855
of themselves.

44
00:02:33,405 --> 00:02:38,305
Here's what disclosure actually looks like on a real ad, not as an abstract

45
00:02:38,305 --> 00:02:41,311
requirement but as one line of on-screen text.

46
00:02:41,311 --> 00:02:43,336
The clip itself doesn't change.

47
00:02:43,336 --> 00:02:48,367
Nothing about the script, the delivery, or the assembly from Section 4.5 gets

48
00:02:48,367 --> 00:02:48,890
touched.

49
00:02:48,890 --> 00:02:54,117
What changes is one caption, visible for the whole runtime, stating plainly that

50
00:02:54,117 --> 00:02:55,163
this is AI-made.

51
00:02:55,163 --> 00:03:00,651
Properly flagged in the ad platform, the platform's own label gets applied on top of

52
00:03:00,651 --> 00:03:05,813
that, automatically, the same way a caption carries a claim the way Section 4.5

53
00:03:05,813 --> 00:03:07,904
already taught you to treat one.

54
00:03:07,904 --> 00:03:11,498
Notice what this costs you in practice: almost nothing.

55
00:03:11,498 --> 00:03:16,725
One line of on-screen text and one honest answer to a flag in the ads platform's

56
00:03:16,725 --> 00:03:17,509
upload flow.

57
00:03:17,509 --> 00:03:22,801
Compare that to the cost of skipping it, a rejected ad at best, a real penalty at

58
00:03:22,801 --> 00:03:28,159
worst, and this becomes one of the cheapest checks in this entire section relative

59
00:03:28,159 --> 00:03:30,054
to what it protects you from.

60
00:03:30,604 --> 00:03:35,295
I want to be precise about why this lecture exists at all, rather than folding

61
00:03:35,295 --> 00:03:38,843
disclosure into a single bullet point back in Section four.

62
00:03:38,843 --> 00:03:41,609
A platform account restriction is a real cost.

63
00:03:41,609 --> 00:03:46,600
A penalty under an actual law is a different order of cost entirely, and as of this

64
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:51,591
recording, more than one jurisdiction has started applying real financial penalties

65
00:03:51,591 --> 00:03:53,034
to exactly this failure.

66
00:03:53,034 --> 00:03:57,905
Whatever the exact numbers are by the time you're watching this, the direction is

67
00:03:57,905 --> 00:04:02,416
the one thing worth trusting completely: disclosure moved from optional, to

68
00:04:02,416 --> 00:04:06,084
platform-enforced, to legally required, inside a single year.

69
00:04:06,084 --> 00:04:10,774
Bet on that direction continuing, not on today's specific numbers staying put.

70
00:04:10,774 --> 00:04:15,525
What should you actually do with a trend like that, rather than just noting it?

71
00:04:15,525 --> 00:04:20,576
Build the habit now, while the cost of skipping it is still mostly a rejected ad, so

72
00:04:20,576 --> 00:04:23,703
it's already automatic by the time it's a legal one.

73
00:04:23,703 --> 00:04:27,973
Nobody builds a new habit well under the pressure of an actual penalty.

74
00:04:27,973 --> 00:04:33,024
You build it now, cheaply, while the stakes are still forgiving enough to learn from

75
00:04:33,024 --> 00:04:35,189
a mistake instead of paying for one.

76
00:04:35,739 --> 00:04:40,773
Next lecture takes the rights thread from Sections four and five and finally gives

77
00:04:40,773 --> 00:04:45,745
it the full, systematic treatment it's been owed since the licence-tier gap first

78
00:04:45,745 --> 00:04:47,587
showed up in Harlan's account.

79
00:04:47,587 --> 00:04:50,595
Before that, go check your own current campaigns.

80
00:04:50,595 --> 00:04:55,506
Does every AI-generated or AI-edited asset you have running carry the disclosure

81
00:04:55,506 --> 00:05:00,539
your platform actually requires, right now, not whatever you remember reading when

82
00:05:00,539 --> 00:05:01,767
you first set it up?

83
00:05:01,767 --> 00:05:06,678
If you're not sure, that uncertainty is the actual finding here, worth resolving

84
00:05:06,678 --> 00:05:08,888
today rather than at the next audit.

85
00:05:08,888 --> 00:05:14,044
Write the caption habit down somewhere you'll actually see it, the same brief folder

86
00:05:14,044 --> 00:05:17,789
Section 4.1 already asked you to keep licence screenshots in.

87
00:05:17,789 --> 00:05:22,761
"Made with AI," visible for the full runtime, on every generated or significantly

88
00:05:22,761 --> 00:05:27,794
edited clip, by default, not as a decision you make fresh each time under deadline

89
00:05:27,794 --> 00:05:28,347
pressure.

90
00:05:28,347 --> 00:05:33,319
A habit you don't have to remember to apply is the only kind that survives a busy

91
